> Any statement in the predicate calculus (which includes propositional
> logic, and its grammatically correct or well-formed formulas (WFFs)) can be
> Godelized, that is, each possible statement can be uniquely coded or
> labeled with its Godel number (via a complicated process). Chaitin's scheme
> does the same thing, so in this sense I would say that, yes, all statements
> of the propositional calculus, and hence all WFFs, can be "Chaitinized."

That is not sufficient.  For Chaitin's omega theorem to show what you think
it does, it must not only be the case that all WFFs can be "Chaitinized" but
that the list of WFFs Chaitinized into the digits of omega actually exhausts
all WFFs.  I think you still do not understand this point.

If the omega set does *not* exhaust all WFFs, than the most Chaitin has done
is demonstrate that a particular subset of all WFFs is "random" --- whatever
that means.  While this might be an interesting technical result, it would be no
philosophical revolution (and in particular would not show, as you have
asserted Chaitin's proof does, the sparseness of the set of decideables).
 
Having got you to commit yourself on this issue and defend the proof to the
best of your apparent ability, these will be *my* last words on the subject
until I have dug Chaitin's proof myself.

