   So would I. 

I agree that the test as proposed is fuzzy, and I'd also criticize the
fact that it essentially presupposes a weak form of Turing test, in
that the AI has to be able to communicate well enough to accomplish a
human-equivalent mission (In Rich's scenario, to act enough like a
stereotypical butler to say "I took the liberty of preparing a
lasagne, sir"). We could quibble over how much NLP is enough for this;
it looks to me like a lot, if you want a system that someone could
actually use and depend on, or even one that many people would agree
_demonstrated_ the human-modelling function convincingly.

OTOH, this ties in with the lower vs. higher primates distinction that
was brought up before, where the author of the Scientific American
article showed that monkey communication can be explained without
appeal to one monkey modeling the mental state of another; clearly, we
regard any intelligence as more effective in the world and in
discourse the better it models the world and the other intelligences
it has to interact with. This is a research focus at the MIT Media
Lab, among other places, and a necessary step in the development of
intelligent "assistant" programs. I toured the ML last year, and still
have some good info on projects (and a left-handed coffee mug).

So, sort of like my android sex partners, I'd say that you don't want
to let the superficial silliness of "andropsychotropic" robots get in
the way of appreciating the value of the concept to a broad view of
advanced robotics ("I've taken the liberty of running you a hot bath
and laying out a choice of massage implements for afterward, Ma'am").
