In this connection, *do not miss* this month's SciAm article on autism.

What the authors seem to have established is that a neurological inability
to model "other minds" is what underlies autism.

In fact, hold on while I type in a quote (p. 113):

	Many comparable experiments have been carried out in other
	laboratories, which have largely confirmed our prediction:
	autistic children are specifically impaired in their under-
	standing of mental states.  They appear to lack the innate
	component underlying this ability.  This component, when it
	works normally, has the most far-reaching implications for
	higher-order conscious processes.  It underpins the special
	feature of the human mind, the ability to reflect on itself.
	[...] Autistic individuals [...] lack a theory of mind; they
	cannot understand how behavior is caused by mental states or
	how beliefs and attitudes can be manipulated.  Hence, they
	find it difficult to understand deception.  The psychological
	undercurrents of real life [...] remain a closed book.

The sentence immediately following is what drove it home to me.

	"People talk to each other with their eyes," said one observant
	autistic youth.  "What is it that they are saying?"

Spooky, powerful stuff.  The authors are quite well aware of the connection
to primatology; in fact they *cite* the fascinating article Craig mentioned.
The implication is clear: chimps, gorillas, non-autistic humans and other
higher primates can model other minds --- but autistic humans and lower
primates cannot.

Puts me in mind of something else, too --- Julian Jaynes's insistence on
the "narratizing" function of consciousness as a critical feature of modern
human thought.
