Subject: Meta: Solomonic Wisdom Needed

I've been listening to this discussion of list management with some
amusement.  As is usually the case, those who want to vest overreaching
powers in a single authority seem to imagine that those powers will only
reach to other people.  (I'll credit Tim May, though, for recognizing the
humor in this phenomena.)

Those of you who want to be ruled by a list tzar are free to contract
amongst yourselves to set one up.  Harry, Robin Hanson, and I have
carefully discussed list PPL and have agreed that it can be instituted with
a bare minimum of regulations.  

Those of you who want to set up your own legal institutions can do so
without ever telling Harry--so long as your chosen judge does do not call
on Harry to enforce his or her decisions.  If you want Harry's help you
need only ask him when the need arises.  He will of course ask for proof
that all concerned parties have consented to the jurisdiction of your
private judge, and may ask you to post your enforcement request to a
special address.

It's easy.  It's fun.  It's do-it-yourself authoritarianism!  

But isn't something missing?  Ah, yes, the people who won't agree to the
jurisdiction of your list tzar are probably the very people you want to
squelch.  Isn't that always the problem, though?  Damn those
self-interested freedom lovers!

Less sarcastically, I encourage those of you who seek different modes of
list management to experiment amongst yourselves and help us all to learn
from your experiences.  Maybe you'll convince us that the list should set
up a tzar for everyone.  But I, for one, would need pretty convincing proof
before I would support such a scheme.

